  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 212 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: There may be a few high clouds present at sunrise but \nsunny skies will prevail both today and Sunday. A 1033 mb high \npressure has become well established across the region this morning \nand will remain in place through much of the day Sunday. As a \nresult, winds will be light and variable throughout the day today. \nWinds will shift to the south at 7-12 mph Sunday afternoon as this \nhigh departs to the east and gradient flow begins to pick up. No \nprecipitation is expected this weekend as high temperatures will be \nnear or slightly above seasonal normals.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A cold front is slated to move across the region during \nthe second half of Monday. There are an increasing number of \ndeterministic models and ensemble perturbations that are showing \nincreasing rain chances Monday afternoon and evening across parts of \nthe forecast area. However, the consensus is still favoring a mostly \ndry passage of the cold front. We have introduced some 20-30 PoPs \nfor portions of northern New York and northern Vermont as a result \nbut the antecedent dry air will likely make it very difficult for \nany rain to make it to the surface. Thereafter, temperatures on \nTuesday will be noticeably cooler as highs will struggle to warm \ninto the mid 40s. Another high pressure system, this one even \nstronger than the last, will move overhead on Wednesday which will \nallow the extended period of dry weather to continue. Temperatures \nwill also moderate underneath this high pressure system with temps \nclicking back into the upper 40s to mid 50s on Wednesday. There is a \nchance we could see a frontal boundary approach from the west late \nnext week but looks rather unimpressive at this time.",
